{
    "title": "Delegation of Authority to the Director of the Division of Investment Management",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 31, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-31/pdf/2025-24127.pdf",
    "summary": "Three federal actions close out the year: updated import assessments on certain cotton products, a new internal shift at the Securities and Exchange Commission to speed decisions on confidentiality requests in investment adviser filings, and a one-year extension of pandemic-era telemedicine flexibilities for prescribing controlled medications through December 31, 2026.\n\nImport assessments for raw cotton content in a range of products have been updated, which helps fund research and promotion programs tied to the cotton industry and gives importers clearer rates. The Securities and Exchange Commission now lets the Director of its investment management division issue orders to grant, deny, or revoke requests to keep parts of adviser registration and reporting filings confidential, a change meant to conserve resources and move applications faster. The Drug Enforcement Administration and the Department of Health and Human Services extended, for a fourth time, the flexibility that allows clinicians to prescribe certain controlled medicines via telemedicine without an initial in\u2011person visit, with the extension running from January 1 through December 31, 2026.\n\nWho benefits? Cotton producers and the programs supported by assessments may see steadier funding, and importers get updated, published rates for planning. Investment advisers and Commission staff may benefit from quicker, more predictable decisions on confidentiality requests for items in registration and reporting forms. Patients who rely on telemedicine\u2014especially those in rural areas, with mobility limits, or seeking mental health and addiction treatment\u2014gain another year of easier access to needed medications, and clinicians can continue serving them without disrupting care.\n\nWho might be negatively affected? Importers and retailers of cotton goods could face modest cost increases tied to the updated assessments, which may flow through to consumers. Investor advocates may worry that faster, delegated confidentiality decisions could reduce transparency around adviser disclosures, even though orders remain subject to notice, the possibility of a hearing, and potential Commission review. The continued telemedicine flexibility may raise concerns about misuse or diversion of controlled medications, putting ongoing monitoring burdens on prescribers, pharmacies, and regulators, and patients could still encounter uneven pharmacy policies when filling telemedicine prescriptions.\n\nOverall, these moves aim to streamline agency work, provide certainty to markets, and maintain access to care\u2014while leaving open questions about transparency, costs, and oversight that stakeholders will watch closely in 2026.",
